@@ -0,0 +1,137 @@
+# Ghost preview network traffic
+
+## What changed
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Who draws ghosts | Server, one replicated Part per player in `Workspace.Ghosts` | Each client, with parts that exist only on that client in `Workspace.LocalGhosts` |
+| Client → server | `MoveGhost(blockTypeName, cell)` every `RenderStepped`, even when nothing changed or placing is off | `GhostState(buffer)` only when the cell, block type or visibility changes, at most 20 a second |
+| Server → clients | Property replication of the ghost's `CFrame` (and `Color`/`Material`/`Transparency`) for every change, sent one by one | One `GhostUpdates(buffer)` batch per 50 ms tick to all clients, holding only ghosts that changed. Nothing is sent when no ghost changed |
+| Own ghost | The server's part: you saw it after a full round trip | Local part moved every frame: no network delay |
+| Other players' ghosts | Jumped from cell to cell whenever replication arrived | Glide toward the latest cell every frame (exponential smoothing), and snap on jumps longer than 32 studs |
+
+Wire format (`src/ReplicatedStorage/Shared/GhostCodec.luau`):
+
+- Client state: 7 bytes. `u8` block type index (0 = hidden), then `i16` x, y, z cell indices.
+- Server batch: 8 bytes per changed ghost. `u8` slot, `u8` type index, `i16` x, y, z. The server gives each player a small slot number in the `GhostSlot` attribute, so entries don't have to carry player references.
+
+Block placement (`BlockPlacer`, `BlockPlacement.server.luau`, `PlaceBlock`) is untouched. The
+ghost parts still have `CanQuery = false`, so they never block the placement raycast.
+
+## Estimates
+
+These are paper estimates, not measurements. Roblox doesn't publish its exact wire format, so I
+use round numbers and state each one. The ratios between before and after matter more than the
+absolute bytes. Check them with the Network stats view in Studio (Shift+F3) or the Developer
+Console's network panel during a 12-player test.
+
+### Assumptions
+
+- **Remote event overhead:** about 10 bytes per call (message id, instance reference, argument
+  count), plus a 1-byte type tag per argument.
+- **Argument sizes:** a `Vector3` is 12 bytes of floats. A short string is about 1 length byte
+  plus its characters (block names average about 5). A buffer is about 2 length bytes plus its
+  contents.
+- **Property replication:** one property change costs about 20 bytes (instance id, property id,
+  axis-aligned `CFrame`).
+- **UDP/IP and packet headers:** left out. Roblox packs many messages into each packet it sends
+  per network tick, so these headers stay roughly the same before and after.
+- **Server:** 12 players, all with placing enabled (the default). 9 run at 60 FPS and 3 at
+  240 FPS.
+- **Builders:** "typical" means 4 players actively building. "Worst" means all 12.
+- **Cell change rate:** a builder sweeping the mouse changes cell about 15 times a second on
+  average. Fast flicks can change cell every frame (60 a second or more).
+
+### Before
+
+**Client → server.** Every client fires every frame, whether or not anything changed. One
+message is 10 + (1 + 1 + 5) + (1 + 12) ≈ **30 bytes**.
+
+- One 60 FPS client sends 60 × 30 = 1.8 KB/s. A 240 FPS client sends 240 × 30 = **7.2 KB/s**,
+  as 240 reliable messages a second.
+- Server inbound is 9 × 60 + 3 × 240 = **1,260 messages/s**, and 1,260 × 30 ≈ **37.8 KB/s**
+  (about 300 kbit/s). It doesn't depend on how many people build: idle players cost the same.
+- The server also runs the handler 1,260 times a second.
+
+**Server → clients.** Setting `CFrame` to the value it already has doesn't replicate, so traffic
+only follows real cell changes. Each change goes to all 12 clients (the builder included, who
+needs it to see their own ghost).
+
+- Typical: 4 × 15 changes × 20 B = 1.2 KB/s per client (60 updates/s). Across 12 clients that is
+  **14.4 KB/s**.
+- Worst: 12 builders × 60 changes × 20 B = **14.4 KB/s per client** (720 updates/s). Across 12
+  clients that is **173 KB/s**. It rises further when high-FPS players flick the mouse, since
+  the cap is one change per frame.
+
+What players saw: high-FPS clients pushed hundreds of reliable messages a second. The reliable
+queue backed up, which raised everyone's ping, including other traffic such as `PlaceBlock`.
+Anchored part moves aren't interpolated, so other players' ghosts teleported whenever a backed-up
+burst of updates arrived.
+
+### After
+
+**Client → server.** One message is 10 + 1 + 2 + 7 ≈ **20 bytes**. A client sends only when its
+state changes, and at most 20 times a second.
+
+- Idle or not building: **0 B/s**, plus one message when the ghost hides.
+- Active builder: about 15 messages/s on average (300 B/s), with a hard cap of 20/s
+  (**400 B/s**). Frame rate makes no difference: a 240 FPS player sends the same as a 60 FPS one.
+- Server inbound, typical: 4 × 15 = 60 messages/s ≈ **1.2 KB/s**.
+- Server inbound, worst: 12 × 20 = **240 messages/s ≈ 4.8 KB/s**.
+
+**Server → clients.** At most one batch per 50 ms tick, and none when no ghost changed. One
+batch is 10 + 1 + 2 + 8 × N bytes, where N is the number of ghosts that changed.
+
+- Typical (N = 4): 45 B × 20 = **0.9 KB/s per client** (20 messages/s). Across 12 clients that
+  is **10.8 KB/s**.
+- Worst (N = 12): 109 B × 20 = **2.2 KB/s per client** (20 messages/s). Across 12 clients that
+  is **26 KB/s**.
+- Growth with player count: each extra builder adds 8 bytes to each batch, not another message
+  stream. Each extra player adds one more recipient of the batch. With 24 players all building,
+  that is 205 B × 20 ≈ 4.1 KB/s per client.
+
+### Summary
+
+| | Before (typical / worst) | After (typical / worst) | Change |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Upload from one 240 FPS client | 7.2 KB/s, 240 msg/s | 0.3 / 0.4 KB/s, ≤ 20 msg/s | ~18–24× fewer bytes, 12× fewer messages |
+| Server inbound, all clients | 37.8 KB/s, 1,260 msg/s | 1.2 / 4.8 KB/s, 60 / 240 msg/s | ~8–30× less |
+| Download per client | 1.2 / 14.4 KB/s, 60 / 720 updates/s | 0.9 / 2.2 KB/s, ≤ 20 msg/s | similar when typical, ~6.5× less at worst |
+| Server outbound, all clients | 14.4 / 173 KB/s | 10.8 / 26 KB/s | ~1.3–6.5× less |
+
+The biggest win is on the inbound side. That was the side that scaled with frame rate and kept
+running for idle players, and it's the one that clogged the reliable queues. Downstream bytes in
+the typical case barely change. What does change downstream is the message count: it now has a
+hard upper bound (20 a second) whatever the frame rate, the number of builders, or how wildly
+people move their mice.
+
+## Things players will notice
+
+1. **Your own ghost is instant.** It used to trail your cursor by your ping, because it was the
+   server's part. Now it's drawn locally on the same frame as your cursor.
+2. **Other players' ghosts glide instead of jumping.** They slide smoothly from cell to cell, and
+   while moving they may briefly sit between grid cells. A jump of more than 8 cells (32 studs)
+   still snaps.
+3. **Other players' ghosts lag a little more behind the real cursor.** The added delay on top of
+   ping is up to 50 ms of client send throttle, plus up to 50 ms of waiting for the server's
+   batch tick (about 50 ms on average), plus roughly 30 ms of smoothing. Ping itself should drop
+   a lot, so in practice ghosts should arrive sooner and much more steadily than in the
+   300–400 ms test.
+4. **Fast flicks skip cells for observers.** If a builder crosses several cells within 50 ms,
+   other players see the ghost glide straight to the final cell rather than visit each one. The
+   builder's own ghost still visits every cell.
+5. **Ghosts are no longer in the server's Workspace.** `Workspace.Ghosts` and the `MoveGhost`
+   remote are gone. Each client has its own `Workspace.LocalGhosts` folder, and the remotes are
+   now `GhostState` and `GhostUpdates`. Nothing else in the project used the old names.
+6. **Joining and leaving.** A player who joins gets a snapshot of every visible ghost right after
+   their client first reports its own state. A player's ghost disappears within one tick (50 ms)
+   of them leaving.
+
+## Tuning
+
+- `GhostCodec.SendRate` (20) controls both the client send cap and the server batch rate. At 30,
+  observers lag less, and the worst-case downstream rises to about 3.3 KB/s per client. At 10,
+  traffic halves, and glides become more visible.
+- `SMOOTHING` (30) in `GhostPreview.client.luau`: lower values glide more softly but lag more.
+  If you change `SendRate`, keep `SMOOTHING` at about 1.5 × `SendRate`, so a ghost has mostly
+  reached each cell before the next update arrives.
